Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study was a small retrospective single-center cohort. Patients lacking perioperative creatinine data and those who died within 48 hours after surgery were excluded; excluding early deaths may remove the sickest recipients before complete AKI ascertainment, and the numbers excluded for each criterion were not reported.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The final predictors were routinely measured preoperative or intraoperative variables available before the 7-day outcome window. Hemodynamic measurements were collected objectively through invasive arterial and Swan-Ganz monitoring, with MAP and CVP recorded every 5 minutes and transformed using prespecified thresholds.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Stage 2–3 AKI was defined using an objective KDIGO-based serum-creatinine criterion—at least a twofold rise within 7 days—with baseline creatinine taken from the most recent measurement within 48 hours before surgery.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Only 25 outcome events were available for an eight-predictor final model. Univariate screening, LASSO selection, KNN imputation, and normalization were not explicitly nested within the cross-validation folds, creating potential information leakage. Hyperparameter tuning and performance estimation used the same five-fold cross-validation framework, with no independent test set, external validation, or AUC confidence interval.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates explainable machine-learning models for predicting an important early complication after heart transplantation using perioperative recipient and hemodynamic data. It is directly applicable to AI research in thoracic organ transplantation.

